1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,156
Every day, your desk has to buy tomorrow’s
supplemental electricity before

2
00:00:05,156 --> 00:00:11,250
you know how much wind will actually
arrive. Buy too little, and a

3
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:16,875
forecast miss becomes an uncovered
position. Buy too much, and you carry

4
00:00:16,875 --> 00:00:21,562
unnecessary hedge volume. This pilot
connects those decisions: the wind

5
00:00:21,562 --> 00:00:26,719
forecast available when you committed, the
uncertainty around it, and the

6
00:00:26,719 --> 00:00:30,000
thermal procurement required for each
delivery period.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,735
For this pilot, we use eleven UTC on the
previous day as the

8
00:00:35,735 --> 00:00:40,147
desk’s decision cutoff. That is
configurable, rather than a universal

9
00:00:40,147 --> 00:00:43,235
exchange deadline. The essential question
is what

10
00:00:43,235 --> 00:00:45,000
information was available then.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,373
Let’s select January first and lock the
previous day’s eleven UTC cutoff.

12
00:00:50,373 --> 00:00:55,299
The forecast curve now represents the
latest eligible publications for each

13
00:00:55,299 --> 00:00:59,776
delivery timestamp. Updates published
after that cutoff cannot influence the

14
00:00:59,776 --> 00:01:03,806
recommendation. I can compare that
decision-time view with actual

15
00:01:03,806 --> 00:01:08,284
generation, then inspect the underlying
settlement row. We are evaluating

16
00:01:08,284 --> 00:01:13,209
the forecast the trader could have used,
rather than a revision

17
00:01:13,209 --> 00:01:15,000
published after the decision.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,538
Now I set expected demand to fifteen
thousand megawatts and select P90

19
00:01:20,538 --> 00:01:25,154
protection. For each half-hour, the
calculator subtracts forecast wind and

20
00:01:25,154 --> 00:01:29,769
adds a buffer calibrated from earlier
forecast errors. Negative procurement

21
00:01:29,769 --> 00:01:34,846
is clipped to zero. For this settlement
period, the recommendation is

22
00:01:34,846 --> 00:01:39,000
[INSERT_METRIC] megawatts. This gives the
trader an explicit scenario

23
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,154
position, with demand and risk appetite
visible instead of

24
00:01:43,154 --> 00:01:45,000
buried inside a spreadsheet.

25
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The headline thermal card averages the
individual recommendations; it is not

26
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,455
one quantity to buy for every half-hour.
The settlement ledger supplies the

27
00:01:55,455 --> 00:01:59,091
actual period-level schedule. Hovering
reveals publication provenance and

28
00:01:59,091 --> 00:02:04,091
the historical sample count behind the
buffer. Export carries those details

29
00:02:04,091 --> 00:02:10,000
into the review process. Before applying
this to a real portfolio, we must

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,000
also align demand, existing supply, and
the coverage of metered wind.

31
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,077
The risk lab separates average accuracy
from trading direction. Our monthly

32
00:02:20,077 --> 00:02:25,154
MAE is [INSERT_METRIC] megawatts, RMSE is
[INSERT_METRIC], and mean bias is

33
00:02:25,154 --> 00:02:29,769
[INSERT_METRIC]. MAE describes the typical
absolute miss; RMSE gives more

34
00:02:29,769 --> 00:02:35,308
weight to large misses. Bias is forecast
minus actual. Positive errors mean

35
00:02:35,308 --> 00:02:40,846
we expected wind that did not arrive.
Negative errors mean the wind

36
00:02:40,846 --> 00:02:45,000
component required less supplemental
procurement than the forecast implied.

37
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,615
Over-forecasting can expose a short
position to adverse imbalance prices;

38
00:02:49,615 --> 00:02:54,231
under-forecasting can create excess
hedging cost. Monthly P90 shortfall is

39
00:02:54,231 --> 00:02:57,923
[INSERT_METRIC] megawatts, measured only
among positive errors. The

40
00:02:57,923 --> 00:03:03,000
calculator’s P90 buffer serves a different
purpose: it uses all signed

41
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,077
historical errors available before each
decision, with a zero floor. That

42
00:03:08,077 --> 00:03:12,231
supports disciplined protection of the
position, without promising future

43
00:03:12,231 --> 00:03:15,000
coverage or claiming proven cash savings.

44
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,545
Here we compare forecast accuracy across
lead-time buckets, starting further

45
00:03:19,545 --> 00:03:25,455
out and moving closer to delivery. Read
the observed MAE and RMSE rather

46
00:03:25,455 --> 00:03:30,455
than assuming improvement is perfectly
monotonic. An eleven UTC daily cutoff

47
00:03:30,455 --> 00:03:35,455
spans different lead times across
tomorrow’s settlement periods, so it is

48
00:03:35,455 --> 00:03:40,455
not a single horizon bucket. The intraday
profile then shows whether

49
00:03:40,455 --> 00:03:45,000
particular delivery hours repeatedly
deserve closer attention from the desk.

50
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,286
Across January, the default policy matches
all forty-eight settlement

51
00:03:49,286 --> 00:03:55,000
periods per day. The practical outcome is
a repeatable path from forecast

52
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,810
vintage to procurement recommendation and
audit trail. On

53
00:03:58,810 --> 00:04:01,667
[INSERT_EVENT_DATE], we observed
[INSERT_EVENT_DESCRIPTION]. The chart

54
00:04:01,667 --> 00:04:07,381
shows why the timing and direction of that
miss matter alongside monthly

55
00:04:07,381 --> 00:04:13,095
averages. This is the event I would review
with the desk before

56
00:04:13,095 --> 00:04:15,000
agreeing operational alert thresholds.

57
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,156
Phase two brings three additions:
imbalance prices to translate volume risk

58
00:04:20,156 --> 00:04:23,906
into economic exposure; weather ensembles
to strengthen uncertainty

59
00:04:23,906 --> 00:04:28,594
estimates; and automated alerts when a
position breaches agreed thresholds.

60
00:04:28,594 --> 00:04:32,812
We would validate performance through a
controlled shadow-trading period

61
00:04:32,812 --> 00:04:37,031
before connecting execution. My
recommendation is to progress with

62
00:04:37,031 --> 00:04:41,719
desk-approved risk limits and a jointly
agreed scorecard covering forecast

63
00:04:41,719 --> 00:04:45,000
quality, excess hedging, and actual
imbalance cost.

64
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,625
Switching now to the technical handover:
the frontend is React with Vite,

65
00:04:50,625 --> 00:04:54,375
Tailwind, and Recharts. FastAPI owns
validation, forecast selection,

66
00:04:54,375 --> 00:04:59,531
analytics, and CSV export. The browser
sends scenario parameters rather than

67
00:04:59,531 --> 00:05:04,219
