Docker separates the two services.

68
00:05:04,219 --> 00:05:08,906
Configuration targets a Vercel frontend
and Render API; this demonstration

69
00:05:08,906 --> 00:05:13,125
runs locally, so I am not presenting an
unverified

70
00:05:13,125 --> 00:05:15,000
cloud deployment as completed.

71
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,000
The loader calls the public FUELHH and
WINDFOR streams without an API key.

72
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:26,077
It keeps wind actuals and every forecast
vintage, normalizes UTC timestamps,

73
00:05:26,077 --> 00:05:30,231
and removes boundary duplicates. Daily
downloads use bounded concurrency,

74
00:05:30,231 --> 00:05:34,846
timeouts, retries, and resumable chunks. A
checksum identifies the snapshot.

75
00:05:34,846 --> 00:05:39,923
Replacement is atomic and requires
complete January actual coverage, so a

76
00:05:39,923 --> 00:05:43,154
failed refresh cannot overwrite the
previous complete

77
00:05:43,154 --> 00:05:45,000
dataset with partial results.

78
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,217
The critical invariant is publication time
at or before the decision cutoff.

79
00:05:50,217 --> 00:05:54,565
For each delivery timestamp, the engine
searches its ordered forecast

80
00:05:54,565 --> 00:05:59,348
vintages and selects the latest eligible
record. The semantics are a

81
00:05:59,348 --> 00:06:03,696
backward as-of join, implemented here with
timestamp indexes and binary

82
00:06:03,696 --> 00:06:07,609
search. Eligibility includes an exactly
matching cutoff. Historical buffer

83
00:06:07,609 --> 00:06:11,522
calibration separately applies the same
availability rule to actual

84
00:06:11,522 --> 00:06:15,000
publications, preventing later corrections
from entering earlier decisions.

85
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,091
The download begins in December,
preserving January first’s prior-day

86
00:06:19,091 --> 00:06:24,091
forecasts and enough history for
calibration. It extends into February to

87
00:06:24,091 --> 00:06:27,727
capture January’s final actual
publication. Exact delivery timestamps

88
00:06:27,727 --> 00:06:31,818
take priority. Missing half-hours can be
linearly interpolated between

89
00:06:31,818 --> 00:06:36,364
neighboring hourly forecasts, but both
endpoints must satisfy the target

90
00:06:36,364 --> 00:06:42,273
cutoff. Gaps longer than one hour remain
missing, and we never extrapolate a

91
00:06:42,273 --> 00:06:45,000
forecast beyond its available delivery
range.

92
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,030
Caching serves two purposes: reproducible
analysis and independence from

93
00:06:49,030 --> 00:06:53,507
